WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.722
This chapter covers the airway clearance and lung expansion techniques on the study guide.

2
00:00:07.722 --> 00:00:19.519
We start with manual chest physiotherapy: postural drainage, percussion and vibration, and the sputum numbers that tell you when to start and stop.

3
00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:28.667
Then mechanical percussion, the high-frequency chest wall vest, intrapulmonary percussive ventilation and Metaneb.

4
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:35.889
Then positive expiratory pressure and the vibratory devices, the Flutter and the Acapella.

5
00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:41.025
We finish with IPPB, and why the deck says it has been replaced.

6
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:51.238
Chest physiotherapy, also called postural drainage, percussion and vibration, loosens and clears secretions.

7
00:00:51.238 --> 00:01:00.042
Postural drainage uses gravity: the affected segment is placed up, so mucus moves toward the larger airways.

8
00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:03.221
Hold each position five to ten minutes.

9
00:01:03.221 --> 00:01:13.330
Sessions run twenty to forty minutes, before meals or two hours after; Hess says more than one hour after, but use the deck.

10
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:18.547
Percussion is rapid thumping with cupped hands over the segment.

11
00:01:18.547 --> 00:01:23.683
Vibration is rapid shaking of the chest wall during expiration.

12
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:31.395
Sputum production above thirty milliliters a day indicates airway clearance.

13
00:01:31.395 --> 00:01:40.921
Stop postural drainage when the sputum drops below thirty milliliters a day and the patient has an effective cough.

14
00:01:40.921 --> 00:01:47.961
Hess says less than twenty five milliliters a day does not justify postural drainage.

15
00:01:47.961 --> 00:01:56.410
Patients with cystic fibrosis are generally no longer placed head-down, because of the risk of reflux.

16
00:01:56.410 --> 00:02:07.841
The deck's contraindications are an unstable head injury, recent spinal surgery, uncontrolled hypertension and certain cardiac conditions.

17
00:02:07.841 --> 00:02:21.425
Hess adds no head-down position with gross hemoptysis, and that percussion can worsen burns, open wounds, skin grafts, subcutaneous emphysema or a recent pacemaker.

18
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:38.428
Handheld percussors and vibrators, pneumatic or electrical, give a consistent rate and force with no fatigue for the user, and the RCP 203 deck calls them equally effective as cupped hands.

19
00:02:38.428 --> 00:02:46.596
Hess says they are more convenient, but data are lacking that they improve clearance; use the deck.

20
00:02:46.596 --> 00:03:01.531
High-frequency chest wall oscillation uses an inflatable vest and an air-pulse generator that makes rapid positive-pressure bursts, adjustable from five to twenty hertz in the deck.

21
00:03:01.531 --> 00:03:09.534
It is used mainly for cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis, especially at home without a caregiver.

22
00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:20.508
Hess says the vest presses five to twenty centimeters of water on the chest, makes mini-coughs, and runs at two to twenty five hertz.

23
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:37.193
Intrapulmonary percussive ventilation delivers high-flow mini-bursts with an aerosol, at three hundred to four hundred pulses per minute, and the deck says it replaced IPPB.

24
00:03:37.193 --> 00:03:41.075
It has its own chapter later in this series.

25
00:03:41.075 --> 00:03:56.864
Metaneb, in RCP 203, combines continuous positive expiratory pressure for lung expansion with continuous high-frequency oscillation for secretion clearance, along with an aerosol.

26
00:03:56.864 --> 00:04:05.508
A session runs about ten minutes, alternating the modes, and it can run in line with a ventilator.

27
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:18.829
Positive expiratory pressure devices typically make six to twenty five centimeters of water during exhalation, while inspiration is unrestricted.

28
00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:26.130
PEP also helps prevent or treat atelectasis, and reduces air trapping in asthma and COPD.

29
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:32.283
To mobilize secretions, a vibratory PEP device is probably the best choice.

30
00:04:32.283 --> 00:04:42.128
The technique: sit upright or semi-Fowler's, start at the lowest PEP and raise it to ten to twenty centimeters of water.

31
00:04:42.128 --> 00:04:53.941
Do sets of ten to twenty slow, moderately deep breaths with short holds and active, not forced, exhalation, and a directed cough after each set.

32
00:04:53.941 --> 00:04:59.438
Repeat four to eight times, in a session of twenty minutes or less.

33
00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:02.883
Hess says four to six times; use the deck.

34
00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.838
The two vibratory PEP devices vibrate in different ways.

35
00:05:08.838 --> 00:05:11.269
The Flutter uses a steel ball.

36
00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:22.532
It makes ten to twenty five centimeters of water in the deck, about ten in Hess, and the pressure rises with the device angle and the flow.

37
00:05:22.532 --> 00:05:27.799
Hess notes that it is gravity dependent, so its position matters.

38
00:05:27.799 --> 00:05:33.066
The Acapella uses a counterweighted lever, or plug, and a magnet.

39
00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:41.331
Turning the knob clockwise raises the pressure, and Hess notes that it works independently of gravity.

40
00:05:41.331 --> 00:05:59.725
PEP is contraindicated if the patient cannot tolerate more work of breathing, with hemodynamic instability, bullous emphysema, high intracranial pressure, an untreated pneumothorax, sinusitis, nosebleeds or middle ear problems.

41
00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:02.658
Pause and decide.

42
00:06:02.658 --> 00:06:11.805
A patient needs to mobilize secretions and will use the device lying back in bed, not sitting upright.

43
00:06:11.805 --> 00:06:17.633
Which vibratory PEP device works regardless of position, and why?

44
00:06:18.533 --> 00:06:24.533
[6-second practice pause]

45
00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:26.092
The Acapella.

46
00:06:26.092 --> 00:06:36.267
It vibrates with a counterweighted lever and a magnet, so Hess notes that it works independently of gravity, in any position.

47
00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:45.628
The Flutter vibrates with a steel ball, so it is gravity dependent, and its pressure changes with the device angle.

48
00:06:45.628 --> 00:06:54.908
For secretion mobilization, a vibratory PEP device is probably the best choice, so the Acapella fits this patient.

49
00:06:56.333 --> 00:07:08.576
Intermittent positive pressure breathing is short-term or episodic mechanical ventilation, used to assist ventilation and give short hyperinflation.

50
00:07:08.576 --> 00:07:23.134
The deck says it was used for lung expansion, but it has been removed from NBRC exams, is no longer recommended, and has been replaced by intrapulmonary percussive ventilation.

51
00:07:23.134 --> 00:07:25.120
Hess still describes it.

52
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:36.535
It is a pneumatically driven, pressure-triggered, pressure-cycled ventilator: the patient triggers it and it passively inflates the lungs.

53
00:07:36.535 --> 00:07:51.176
Set the flow for an I to E ratio of about one to three, aim for breaths at least twenty five percent larger than the patient's tidal volume, and run no more than twenty minutes.

54
00:07:51.176 --> 00:08:05.486
Hess's indications are atelectasis, a cough too weak to work, neuromuscular disease or kyphoscoliosis, and only after other options are exhausted, never for aerosol therapy.

55
00:08:05.486 --> 00:08:08.133
Where they differ, use the deck.

56
00:08:09.533 --> 00:08:10.307
One more.

57
00:08:10.307 --> 00:08:19.930
A patient on postural drainage now produces about twenty milliliters of sputum a day and has an effective cough.

58
00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:25.858
What should you recommend, and what sputum volume justified starting?

59
00:08:26.767 --> 00:08:32.767
[6-second practice pause]

60
00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:34.835
Recommend stopping.

61
00:08:34.835 --> 00:08:47.380
The deck says to stop postural drainage when sputum drops below thirty milliliters a day and the patient has an effective cough, and both are true here.

62
00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:54.974
Sputum above thirty milliliters a day is what indicated airway clearance in the first place.

63
00:08:54.974 --> 00:09:07.767
Hess draws the line slightly lower, saying that under twenty five milliliters a day does not justify postural drainage, and this patient is below that too.

64
00:09:09.167 --> 00:09:11.621
Here is the chapter in brief.

65
00:09:11.621 --> 00:09:23.637
Postural drainage puts the affected segment up, five to ten minutes per position, with percussion by cupped hands and vibration on expiration.

66
00:09:23.637 --> 00:09:31.591
Start above thirty milliliters of sputum a day, and stop below thirty with an effective cough.

67
00:09:31.591 --> 00:09:40.984
Mechanical percussors are as effective as hands, and the vest suits cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis at home.

68
00:09:40.984 --> 00:09:53.593
PEP runs ten to twenty centimeters of water in sets of breaths with a cough after each, and vibratory PEP is probably the best choice for secretions.

69
00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:58.163
The Flutter depends on gravity; the Acapella does not.

70
00:09:58.163 --> 00:10:01.717
And IPPB is off the exam, replaced by IPV.
